 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT in relation to terms and conditions of employment of certain\nnonjudicial officers and employees of the unified court system; to amend\nchapter 400 of the laws of 2014 relating to terms and conditions of employment\nof certain nonjudicial officers and employees of the unified court system, in\nrelation to annual longevity payments for certain employees; and to repeal\ncertain provisions of such chapter relating thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "S1521",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S1521-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Rpld §3 sub (i), amd §3, Chap 400 of 2014",
    "lawSection": "Civil Service",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S1521\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN-SIGAL\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act in relation to terms and conditions of employment of certain\nnonjudicial officers and employees of the unified court system; to amend\nchapter 400 of the laws of 2014 relating to terms and conditions of\nemployment of certain nonjudicial officers and employees of the unified\ncourt system, in relation to annual longevity payments for certain\nemployees; and to repeal certain provisions of such chapter relating\nthereto\n\nThis measure is being introduced at the request of the Judiciary. It\nimplements collective bargaining agreements between the State and public\nemployee unions negotiating on behalf of approximately 13,400 court\nemployees in all 12 of the Judiciary's negotiating units, including:\nCitywide Law Assistants; Nassau County; New York City Administrative,\nLibrarian, Clerical, and Support; New York City Administrative Services;\nNew York City Court Clerks; New York City Court Officers; New York City\nCourt Reporters; New York City Senior Court Attorneys; New York City\nSenior Court Officers; Ninth Judicial District; State Judiciary; and\nSuffolk County. These collective bargaining agreements provide salary\nincreases and other benefits for the period from April 1, 2021 through\nMarch 31, 2026. This measure also authorizes like salary increases and\nother benefits for the same period for approximately 1,150 State-paid\nunrepresented nonjudicial employees of the Unified Court System (\"UCS\").\n\nThis measure (Bill Sections 3 and 4) authorizes the following for\naffected represented nonjudicial officers and employees during the peri-\nod covered by the agreements (and for unrepresented nonjudicial officers\nand employees during the same period):\n\n- Effective retroactively to April 1, 2021, all employees shall receive\nan increment, if eligible therefor and if they have not already received\nsuch increment(1), and a pay increase of 2% (or $1,000, whichever is\ngreater)(2). All employees having at least 20 years of continuous\nservice in the employ of the Unified Court System who are not recipients\nof an annual longevity payment also shall receive a pensionable bonus,\nif they have not already received such bonus(3), in an amount as\nfollows:  $2,100 (where they have between 20 and 25 years of service),\n$2,200 (where they have between 25 and 30 years of service), or $2,300\n(where they have 30 or more years of service). And employees at or above\nthe maximum grade of their respective positions shall receive annual\nlongevity payments where qualified pursuant to section 3(i) of chapter\n400 of the laws of 2014.\n\n- Effective retroactively to April 1, 2022, all employees shall receive\nan increment, if eligible therefor and if they have not already received\nsuch increment, and a pay increase of 2% (or $1,000, whichever is great-\ner). All employees having at least 20 years of continuous service in the\nemploy of the Unified Court System who are not recipients of an annual\nlongevity payment also shall receive a pensionable bonus, if they have\nnot already received such bonus, in an amount as follows: $2,200 (where\nthey have between 20 and 25 years of service), $2,300 (where they have\nbetween 25 and 30 years of service), or $2,400 (where they have 30 or\nmore years of service).  And employees at or above the maximum grade of\ntheir respective positions shall receive annual longevity payments where\nqualified pursuant to section 3(i) of chapter 400 of the laws of 2014.\n- Effective April 1, 2023, all employees shall receive an increment, if\neligible therefor, and a pay increase of 3%. All employees having at\nleast 20 years of continuous service in the employ of the Unified Court\nSystem who are not recipients of an annual longevity payment also shall\nreceive a pensionable bonus in an amount as follows:  $2,270 (where they\nhave between 20 and 25 years of service), $2,370 (where they have\nbetween 25 and 30 years of service), or $2,475 (where they have 30 or\nmore years of service). And employees at or above the maximum grade of\ntheir respective positions shall receive annual longevity payments where\nqualified pursuant to section 3(i) of chapter 400 of the laws of 2014.\n\n- Effective April 1, 2024, all employees shall receive an increment, if\neligible therefor, and a pay increase of 3%. All employees having at\nleast 20 years of continuous service in the employ of the Unified Court\nSystem who are not recipients of an annual longevity payment also shall\nreceive a pensionable bonus in an amount as follows:  $2,340 (where they\nhave between 20 and 25 years of service), $2,440 (where they have\nbetween 25 and 30 years of service), or $2,550 (where they have 30 or\nmore years of service). And employees at or above the maximum grade of\ntheir respective positions shall receive annual longevity payments where\nqualified pursuant to section 3(i) of chapter 400 of the laws of 2014.\n\n- Effective April 1, 2025, all employees shall receive an increment, if\neligible therefor, and a pay increase of 3%. All employees having at\nleast 20 years of continuous service in the employ of the Unified Court\nSystem who are not recipients of an annual longevity payment also shall\nreceive a pensionable bonus in an amount as follows:  $2,410 (where they\nhave between 20 and 25 years of service), $2,515 (where they have\nbetween 25 and 30 years of service), or $2,630 (where they have 30 or\nmore years of service). And employees at or above the maximum grade of\ntheir respective positions shall receive annual longevity payments where\nqualified pursuant to section 3(i) of chapter 400 of the laws of 2014.\n\nIn addition to authorizing the foregoing pay increases, this measure\nprovides for the following:\n\n- (Bill Section 5) a one-time lump sum payment of $3,000 to all repres-\nented and unrepresented nonjudicial officers and employees.  This\npayment will not be added to annual salary but it is pensionable.\n\n-(Bill Section 6) pensionable annual location pay for certain affected\nnonjudicial officers and employees, as follows:\n\n- for officers and employees whose principal place of employment is in\nNew York City or in Nassau, Suffolk, Rockland, or Westchester County:\n(i) $4,300 during the fiscal year commencing April 1, 2021, less any\nlocation pay already received pursuant to law; (ii) $4,500 during the\nfiscal year commencing April 1, 2022, less any location pay already\nreceived pursuant to law; (iii) $4,635 during the fiscal year commencing\nApril 1, 2023; (iv) $4,775 during the fiscal year commencing April 1,\n2024; and (v) $4,920 during the fiscal year commencing April 1, 2025 and\nsubsequent fiscal years.\n\n-for officers and employees whose principal place of employment is in\nDutchess, Putnam, or Orange County: (i) $2,150 during the fiscal year\ncommencing April 1, 2021, less any location pay already received pursu-\nant to law; (ii) $2,250 during the fiscal year commencing April 1, 2022,\nless any location pay already received pursuant to law; (iii) $2,318\nduring the fiscal year commencing April 1, 2023; (iv) $2,388 during the\nfiscal year commencing April 1, 2024; (v) $2,460 during the fiscal year\ncommencing April 1, 2021 and subsequent fiscal years.\n\n-for officers and employees whose principal place of employment is in\nMonroe County and who were entitled to receive annual location pay\npursuant to section 5(a)(3) of chapter 400 of the Laws of 2014: the\namount of such pay as was authorized by such section during each fiscal\nyear commencing on or after April 1, 2021, less any location pay already\nreceived pursuant to law.\n\n-(Bill Section 7) a pensionable security and law enforcement differen-\ntial payment for employees in security series titles who (i) have served\nthe equivalent of 120 workdays in a title in such security series during\nthe state fiscal year preceding that date, and (ii) have received other\nthan an unsatisfactory performance rating during the preceding year.\nSuch differential shall be paid annually and shall equal the following:\n\n- $750, effective April 1, 2021, less any differential already received\npursuant to law.\n\n- $800, effective April 1, 2022, less any differential already received\npursuant to law.\n\n- $825, effective April 1, 2023.\n\n- $850, effective April 1, 2024.\n\n- $875, effective April 1, 2025 and each April 1st thereafter.\n\n- (Bill Section 8) a program pursuant to which nonjudicial officers and\nemployees may exchange accumulated annual or comp time leave in return\nfor a cash payment, as follows:\n\n- effective in January 2023 (or within 30 days of the date this measure\nbecomes a law where that date is after January 1st), officers and\nemployees may exchange between 2 and 10 days.\n\n- effective in the month of August in each of the years 2023, 2024, and\n2025, officers and employees may exchange between 2 and 5 days.  Cash\npayments received under this program will not be added to annual salary,\nnor are they pensionable.\n\n- (Bill Section 8-a) provides that any uniform allowance received by\naffected nonjudicial officers and employees shall be pensionable.\n\nThe salary increases and other benefits provided by this measure shall\nnot be implemented for represented nonjudicial officers and employees in\na collective bargaining unit until the Chief Administrative Judge deliv-\ners to the Comptroller a certificate that there is a written collective\nbargaining agreement with the State providing therefor.\n\nFinally, this measure repeals section 3(i) of chapter 400 of the Laws of\n2014 to rescind authority for annual longevity payments after April 1,\n2025.\n\nThis measure would take effect immediately and be deemed to have been in\nfull force and effect on and after April 1, 2021. Implementation of this\nmeasure would cost the Judiciary approximately $157 million in Fiscal\nYear 2023.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY 2021-22: OCA 96\n\n(1) Section 209-a(1) of the Civil Service Law, enacted in 1982 (L. 1982,\nc. 868) bars employers from unilaterally changing the terms and condi-\ntions of employment in an expired collective bargaining agreement until\na new agreement is negotiated. Increments were authorized and paid for\nUCS nonjudicial personnel under prior collective bargaining agreements.\n\n(2) To give effect to this 2% increase and the other percentage pay\nincreases for graded employees provided by this measure, the measure\nprovides for new salary schedules incorporating these increases (Bill\nSection 2).\n\n(3) See footnote 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
